// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/turbobaby/bot_surface.t27 -- the Telegram bot's command surface and its parse rule
; This contract owns five things and nothing else: which commands the bot ACCEPTS, which of them
; carry an admin gate and what that gate actually is, what a start payload carries and what an
; unparseable one does, the callback-data grammar the button router recognises AND THE ORDER IN
; WHICH IT TRIES IT, and the escape rule that keeps a message from breaking its own parse mode.
; Every number is read from src/bot/commands.rs, src/bot/callbacks.rs, src/util.rs, src/promo.rs
; or src/config.rs and carries its line.
; ASCII only (L3), English only (LANG-EN). No shipped message text is quoted anywhere below: the
; reply strings are Cyrillic and are named by path instead.
; --
; WHY (measured 2026-09-21). "The published command surface" is not one number in this
; repository, it is THREE, and they disagree. The parser accepts SIXTEEN commands
; (src/bot/commands.rs:28-71, sixteen description attributes, sixteen dispatch arms at :186-699).
; The help reply advertises FIVE (src/locales.rs:197 and :286, the same five in both published
; locales). And Telegram itself is told NOTHING: grep -rn "set_my_commands" over every .rs in the
; repository returns 0, and so does a grep for descriptions(), so the derive macro's own
; description string is built and never sent. Eleven accepted commands are therefore reachable
; only by typing them blind -- including two customer-facing ones, invite and refstats, whose
; descriptions promise a referral link and referral statistics to a customer who has no way to
; learn they exist. A contract that recorded a single count would have to pick one of the three
; and would be wrong about the other two, which is why the three counts are the first thing
; declared here and why the agreement test asserts that they do NOT agree.
; --
; WHERE THE BRIEF IS REFUTED, first and most importantly. The brief asks for "the measured zero
; this file closes" from grep -ril telegram specs/. That grep returned SIX files, not zero. Five
; were re-opened line by line under review and every one holds a column name, an identity field
; or a D14 privacy field -- telegram_id, telegram_handle -- and not one is a bot surface:
; turbobaby/BOT owns FORBIDDEN_FIELDS, whose first element names telegram handles;
; turbobaby/bike-catalog owns FORBIDDEN_UNIT_FIELDS, which carries telegram_handle as a privacy
; field; turbobaby/game-score owns KEY_COLUMNS_TODAY over the telegram_id primary key of
; migrations/027; turbobaby/order-status refuses a telegram id in its own header; and
; turbobaby/request-identity names one in many places. Owners and declaration names stand here
; instead of line numbers ON PURPOSE: a line number into a sibling goes stale the moment that
; sibling is edited, and this file's siblings have been edited since these five were read.
; The sixth was counted but never named, and the corpus has kept growing since, so it cannot now
; be recovered by name; what a reader can reproduce is the re-run recorded at the end of this
; file -- RE-MEASURED 2026-09-21 at TWENTY-TWO of the other 42 files, not the twelve a mid-wave
; reading gave. The honest zeros are narrower, and all three were re-run on 2026-09-21 and all
; three still hold outside this file: grep -ril "parse_mode" specs/ -> 0, "callback_data" -> 0,
; "BotCommands" -> 0. A grep for a slash-prefixed command name is NO LONGER one of them and is
; no longer claimed as one: it was 0 when this file was written and is 5 of the other 42 today.
; Those are the absences this file closes, and the six-file count is recorded rather
; than rounded down because a reader who repeats the brief's grep must not conclude the survey
; was lying about something else.
; --
; WHERE THE BRIEF IS REFUTED A SECOND TIME, and the ownership edge changes shape. The brief says
; to "name request_identity's ID as the gate owner" for admin commands. The bot does not use that
; gate. src/bot/commands.rs imports nothing from src/api/auth.rs, and a grep of the three bot
; modules for init_data, initData, check_admin and verify_init_data returns two COMMENTS and no
; code (src/bot/mod.rs:105, src/bot/commands.rs:604). The bot's gate is one line repeated six
; times, config.admin_ids.contains(&user_id), against the id Telegram itself put on the webhook
; update. What IS shared is the roster, not the proof: both gates read the same
; Vec<i64> parsed from ADMIN_IDS (src/config.rs:11, :94-98), and the HTTP side reads it at
; src/api/auth.rs:455. So the edge is real but narrower than the brief drew it, and it is
; recorded that way below. The distinction is load-bearing in this repository and not academic:
; src/bot/mod.rs:104-108 states that initData's HMAC "does not currently verify in production",
; which makes the webhook id the STRONGER evidence of the two, not the weaker.
; --
; WHERE THE BRIEF IS REFUTED A THIRD TIME, in its line numbers. The command list is cited as
; :26-70; :26 is the rename_rule attribute and :70 is the description of the sixteenth command,
; whose variant is at :71 and whose enum closes at :72 -- counting the brief's range yields
; fifteen commands. The referral entry is cited as commands.rs:165; :165 is a comment inside the
; pre-dispatch preamble that merely mentions referrals, and the grammar is at :188-190. The
; tripwire is cited as tests/retired_table_wiring.rs:65-73; the survivor entry runs :66-76 (:66-75 until 2026-09-26).
; --
; WHAT THIS FILE NEVER DOES. It quotes no message text, in any language: every reply string the
; bot sends is Cyrillic and is cited by path only, which is also why the strain-of-day format
; string was named as src/bot/callbacks.rs:396-399 (gone: CAROUSEL_RETIRED_AT). It carries no admin id
; and no roster: membership arrives as the boolean id_is_configured_admin and the list stays in
; runtime configuration. It restates no locale set and no picker button --
; turbobaby/locale-policy owns PUBLISHED_SET_REACHES_THE_CUSTOMER_AT, which carries the very
; call sites a reply localises through. It restates no
; order status name, no terminality and no refund predicate: the three order buttons advance an
; order, and turbobaby/order-status owns what they advance it to, down to should_refund_bonus,
; the predicate it pins through REFUND_GUARD_SOURCE. It does not decide whether the cannabis-era surface should
; be retired -- that is a product ruling and legacy-retirement's subject -- it only measures that
; the surface is still wired. It says what the two pre-router callback branches ARE and where in
; the order they sit, and nothing about what they do: the publish and mute buttons of the promo
; draft are promo_broadcast.t27's, down to who may press them. It maps no HTTP path; the read
; transport belongs to catalog_api.t27
; and the roughly eighty-path routing table is not this file's subject under any reading. No
; renter name, handle, phone, key code, plate, debt, purchase price or personal note appears
; here, in constants, fixtures or comments.
; RE-POINTED 2026-09-24: every src/db/mod.rs line this file cites past line 385 moved four lines
; down when migrations/087_delivery_zones_phuket.sql joined MIGRATIONS, and four more with
; migrations/088_unpublish_events.sql the same day; re-measured, and nothing they point at changed.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module turbobaby-bot-surface;

pub const KIND : str = "surface-contract";
pub const ID : str = "turbobaby/bot-surface";
pub const NAME : str = "Telegram bot command surface and callback grammar";
pub const COMMAND_SOURCE : str = "src/bot/commands.rs";
pub const CALLBACK_SOURCE : str = "src/bot/callbacks.rs";
pub const ESCAPE_SOURCE : str = "src/util.rs:2-6";
pub const ROSTER_SOURCE : str = "src/config.rs:11, parsed from ADMIN_IDS at :94-98";
pub const MEASURED_AT : str = "2026-09-21";

; --- Ownership edges ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Five outbound edges, pinned as literals because this language has no import: a brace import
; lowers to an empty UseDecl and fails the gate, so a REUSES_* string plus a test asserting it is
; the only edge a grep can find from both ends. request-identity is the narrowed edge described
; in the header -- shared roster, different proof. locale-policy owns every published locale and
; the picker; a command reply is localised and this file says nothing about how. order-status
; owns the vocabulary three callback buttons write. legacy-retirement owns the ruling on the
; cannabis-era surface measured at the end of this file. promo-broadcast was added under review,
; when the two callback branches that run BEFORE the length bound were measured: this file owns
; where they sit in the routing order, and that file owns what they do.
pub const REUSES_REQUEST_IDENTITY_ID : str = "turbobaby/request-identity";
pub const REUSES_LOCALE_POLICY_ID : str = "turbobaby/locale-policy";
pub const REUSES_ORDER_STATUS_ID : str = "turbobaby/order-status";
pub const REUSES_LEGACY_RETIREMENT_ID : str = "turbobaby/legacy-retirement";
pub const REUSES_PROMO_BROADCAST_ID : str = "turbobaby/promo-broadcast";
pub const REUSE_EDGE_COUNT : u8 = 5;
; That edge is reciprocal without either side being told: turbobaby/promo-broadcast declares
; REUSES_BOT_SURFACE_ID and names this file's callback routing as where the press arrives, and a
; grep of it for the two branch literals, for the cap and for route_callback returns 0 --
; RE-MEASURED 2026-09-21, still 0 on all four. One trap is named because it is exactly how that
; zero gets read wrong: promo_mute, the mute branch's EXACT KEY, is 0 there, while promo_muted,
; the opt-out TABLE of migrations/075, appears 4 times. The two differ by one letter and are
; different subjects, so a substring grep reports four hits and a reader who stops there
; concludes the routing IS restated next door. It is not.
pub const PROMO_BROADCAST_POINTS_BACK_AT : str = "turbobaby/promo-broadcast declares REUSES_BOT_SURFACE_ID";
pub const PROMO_BROADCAST_RESTATES_THE_ROUTING : bool = false;
pub const PROMO_MUTED_TABLE_HITS_NEXT_DOOR : u8 = 4;
pub const PROMO_MUTE_BRANCH_KEY_HITS_NEXT_DOOR : u8 = 0;

; The fourth edge, RE-MEASURED rather than left as written. At the first measurement today
; specs/turbobaby/legacy_retirement.t27 did not exist -- ls returned no such file and a grep of
; specs/ for its ID returned 0 hits outside this file -- so the edge was declared forward,
; because the tripwire below needs an owner to point at and a forward declaration that says so
; beats a measured fact with no addressee. That file has since landed, and the edge is now
; RECIPROCAL: it declares REUSES_BOT_SURFACE_ID = "turbobaby/bot-surface", so a grep finds the
; pair from both ends. The division of labour held on both sides without either being told --
; a grep of that file for sotd, carousel and strain_of_day returns 0, RE-MEASURED 2026-09-21,
; so it rules on the
; retirement, this file measures the wiring, and no table is restated between them.
pub const LEGACY_RETIREMENT_EDGE_WAS_FORWARD_DECLARED : bool = true;
pub const LEGACY_RETIREMENT_EDGE_IS_RECIPROCAL : bool = true;
pub const LEGACY_RETIREMENT_DECLARES_THIS_FILE_AT : str = "turbobaby/legacy-retirement declares REUSES_BOT_SURFACE_ID";
pub const LEGACY_RETIREMENT_RESTATES_THE_CAROUSEL : bool = false;

; --- The three command surfaces, and their disagreement ------------------------------------------
; ACCEPTED: src/bot/commands.rs:28-71, the BotCommands enum, sixteen variants.
; ADVERTISED: src/locales.rs:197 (ru) and :286 (en), five entries each, identical sets.
; REGISTERED: zero. grep -rn "set_my_commands" --include=*.rs over the repository returns 0.
pub const ACCEPTED_COMMAND_COUNT : u8 = 16;
pub const HELP_ADVERTISED_COMMAND_COUNT : u8 = 5;
pub const TELEGRAM_REGISTERED_COMMAND_COUNT : u8 = 0;
pub const SET_MY_COMMANDS_CALL_SITES : u8 = 0;

; Names as the parser sees them. rename_rule = "snake_case" at src/bot/commands.rs:26 is the
; reason the sixteenth is promo_on and not promoon; every other variant is one lowercase word,
; for which the rule changes nothing. Order is the enum's own.
pub const COMMANDS : [16]str = [
    "start", "menu", "joke", "fact",
    "help", "lang", "engage", "factpost",
    "invite", "refstats", "admin", "unblock",
    "blocks", "errors", "promo", "promo_on",
];

; The five the help reply names. The help command is NOT among them: the command that exists to
; list the commands does not list itself.
pub const HELP_ADVERTISED_COMMANDS : [5]str = ["start", "menu", "joke", "fact", "lang"];

pub fn command_is_accepted(name: str) bool {
    var i : u8 = 0;
    while (i < COMMANDS.len) {
        if (COMMANDS[i] == name) {
            return true;
        }
        i += @as(u8, 1);
    }
    return false;
}

pub fn command_is_advertised_by_help(name: str) bool {
    var i : u8 = 0;
    while (i < HELP_ADVERTISED_COMMANDS.len) {
        if (HELP_ADVERTISED_COMMANDS[i] == name) {
            return true;
        }
        i += @as(u8, 1);
    }
    return false;
}

; Nothing is registered with Telegram, so the slash menu shows nothing and discoverability
; collapses onto the help reply. The branch is written rather than folded away so that a future
; set_my_commands call flips this file by changing one constant.
pub fn command_is_discoverable(name: str) bool {
    if (TELEGRAM_REGISTERED_COMMAND_COUNT >= 1) {
        return command_is_accepted(name);
    }
    return command_is_advertised_by_help(name);
}

pub fn undiscoverable_command_count() u8 {
    var found : u8 = 0;
    var i : u8 = 0;
    while (i < COMMANDS.len) {
        if (!command_is_discoverable(COMMANDS[i])) {
            found += @as(u8, 1);
        }
        i += @as(u8, 1);
    }
    return found;
}

pub fn command_surface_counts_agree(accepted: u8, advertised: u8, registered: u8) bool {
    return accepted == advertised and advertised == registered;
}

; --- Who may run a command -----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Six commands carry the gate, each as the same single line. Gate sites, in the order of the
; array below: engage src/bot/commands.rs:443, factpost :511, unblock :705, blocks :623,
; errors :644, promo :666. The gate is membership in the configured roster and nothing else; the
; boolean arrives here already decided, exactly as the corpus does everywhere else.
pub const ADMIN_GATED_COMMAND_COUNT : u8 = 6;
pub const ADMIN_GATED_COMMANDS : [6]str = [
    "engage", "factpost", "unblock", "blocks", "errors", "promo",
];
pub const BOT_GATE_IS_ROSTER_MEMBERSHIP : bool = true;
pub const BOT_GATE_VERIFIES_INIT_DATA : bool = false;
pub const ROSTER_IS_SHARED_WITH_THE_HTTP_GATE : bool = true;

pub fn command_is_admin_gated(name: str) bool {
    var i : u8 = 0;
    while (i < ADMIN_GATED_COMMANDS.len) {
        if (ADMIN_GATED_COMMANDS[i] == name) {
            return true;
        }
        i += @as(u8, 1);
    }
    return false;
}

pub fn bot_admin_command_allowed(command_is_gated: bool, id_is_configured_admin: bool) bool {
    if (!command_is_gated) {
        return true;
    }
    return id_is_configured_admin;
}

; THE ONE DISAGREEMENT BETWEEN WHAT A COMMAND SAYS AND WHAT IT DOES. Seven commands describe
; themselves as privileged: the six above are marked as admin commands and the admin command is
; marked owners-only (src/bot/commands.rs:60). Six are gated. admin is not, and the code says why
; at :603-606 -- its handler only sends a Mini App button, and the screen behind it gates on a
; shared password rather than on the roster. That is a deliberate decision and is recorded as
; one, not smoothed away: a reader who counts privileged descriptions gets seven and a reader who
; counts guards gets six, and the function below is what keeps the pair from drifting unnoticed.
pub const COMMANDS_DESCRIBED_AS_PRIVILEGED : u8 = 7;
pub const DESCRIBED_PRIVILEGED_BUT_UNGATED : str = "admin";
pub const ITS_SCREEN_GATES_ELSEWHERE : bool = true;

pub fn command_is_described_as_privileged(name: str) bool {
    return command_is_admin_gated(name) or name == DESCRIBED_PRIVILEGED_BUT_UNGATED;
}

pub fn description_and_gate_agree(name: str) bool {
    return command_is_described_as_privileged(name) == command_is_admin_gated(name);
}

; --- What the start command carries ---------------------------------------------------------------
; Four payload shapes, tested in source order. The referral branch is first (:188) and is the
; only one that does not return: it records and falls through, so the reply a referred customer
; sees is decided by the two branches after it, at :216 and :232.
pub const START_PAYLOAD_REFERRAL : u8 = 1;
pub const START_PAYLOAD_CHANNEL : u8 = 2;
pub const START_PAYLOAD_MINIAPP : u8 = 3;
pub const START_PAYLOAD_PLAIN : u8 = 4;
pub const START_PAYLOAD_SHAPE_COUNT : u8 = 4;

; src/bot/commands.rs:188-190. The grammar is a literal prefix, then a code, and the only check
; on the code is a length bound; the code itself is looked up rather than parsed.
pub const REFERRAL_PREFIX : str = "ref_";
pub const REFERRAL_CODE_MAX_LEN : u16 = 200;

; src/trios/deeplink.rs:262-263 states it in words and is the single parser both sides share: a
; referral payload is deliberately NOT a Mini App target, so it can never reach the :232 branch.
pub const REFERRAL_PAYLOAD_IS_A_MINIAPP_TARGET : bool = false;

pub fn referral_code_is_within_cap(code_len: u16) bool {
    return code_len <= REFERRAL_CODE_MAX_LEN;
}

; Four conditions, all required. A self-referral is refused at :199 by comparing the two ids, and
; the recording call at :201 is idempotent, so the fourth condition is about correctness of
; attribution rather than about double counting.
pub fn start_records_a_referral(has_prefix: bool, code_within_cap: bool, referrer_found: bool, referrer_is_the_sender: bool) bool {
    return has_prefix and code_within_cap and referrer_found and !referrer_is_the_sender;
}

pub fn start_reply_shape(is_channel: bool, is_miniapp: bool) u8 {
    if (is_channel) {
        return START_PAYLOAD_CHANNEL;
    }
    if (is_miniapp) {
        return START_PAYLOAD_MINIAPP;
    }
    return START_PAYLOAD_PLAIN;
}

; WHAT AN UNPARSEABLE REFERRAL DOES: nothing visible. An over-long code is warned about in the
; log at :191 and dropped; an unknown code resolves to no referrer at :194-198 and is dropped; a
; self-referral is dropped at :199. None of the three answers the customer, none returns early,
; and all three arrive at the same plain welcome a bare start would have produced. That is the
; intended behaviour and not a defect -- but it means a broken invite link is indistinguishable
; from a working one from the customer's side, which is a fact this contract states out loud.
pub const AN_UNPARSEABLE_REFERRAL_IS_SILENT : bool = true;
pub const AN_UNPARSEABLE_REFERRAL_STILL_ANSWERS : bool = true;

pub fn start_reply_shape_for_any_referral() u8 {
    return start_reply_shape(false, REFERRAL_PAYLOAD_IS_A_MINIAPP_TARGET);
}

; --- The callback grammar --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE ORDER IS NOT WHAT A READING OF route_callback ALONE SUGGESTS, and this correction was made
; under review. handle_callback answers a button press in FIVE stages, and the length bound is the
; THIRD of them, not the first:
;   1. src/bot/callbacks.rs:139-157 -- a publish PREFIX, matched on q.data before anything else and
;      returning at :156;
;   2. :162-169 -- a mute EXACT KEY, matched next and returning likewise;
;   3. :171-181 -- the length bound, reached only by data that matched neither;
;   4. :221 route_callback (defined at src/bot/callbacks.rs:76-110) -- five exact keys collapsing
;      to three actions, then six prefixes in a fixed sequence, then Unknown at :109;
;   5. :227 -- the single admin gate over the three order actions.
; Stages 1 and 2 are deliberate and the code says why at :136-138 and :159-161: the publish button
; is the one callback that posts publicly and must not fall through if the routing table changes.
; The consequence is MEASURED rather than inferred, and it is the reason this file cannot describe
; the cap as the first thing tried: those two are the only callback data in this repository acted
; on WITHOUT passing the length bound. What the two buttons DO is REUSES_PROMO_BROADCAST_ID's
; subject and is not described here -- that file already points back at this routing through its
; own REUSES_BOT_SURFACE_ID -- so only the ordering and the bypass are recorded. Handlers are otherwise not
; enumerated: the grammar is what a contract can protect, and the ordering is the part that breaks
; silently, so the ordering is what is pinned.
pub const CALLBACK_ROUTER_STAGE_COUNT : u8 = 5;
pub const PRE_ROUTER_BRANCH_COUNT : u8 = 2;
pub const PRE_ROUTER_PREFIX_SOURCE : str = "src/promo.rs:40, matched at src/bot/callbacks.rs:142";
pub const PRE_ROUTER_EXACT_KEY_SOURCE : str = "src/promo.rs:43, matched at src/bot/callbacks.rs:162";
pub const PRE_ROUTER_BRANCHES_PRECEDE_THE_CAP : bool = true;
pub const THE_CAP_IS_THE_THIRD_STAGE_NOT_THE_FIRST : bool = true;
; src/bot/callbacks.rs:22-23, is_callback_data_valid: data.len() <= 200 on a &str, so the bound is
; in BYTES, and a multi-byte payload reaches it sooner than its character count suggests.
pub const CALLBACK_DATA_MAX_LEN : u16 = 200;
pub const CALLBACK_CAP_IS_MEASURED_IN_BYTES : bool = true;
; The counts below are route_callback's, at stage 4. They are NOT the router's totals: counting the
; two pre-router branches in, a button press is matched against six exact keys and seven prefixes.
pub const CALLBACK_EXACT_KEY_COUNT : u8 = 5;
pub const CALLBACK_EXACT_KEYS : [5]str = [
    "start_joke", "more_joke", "start_fact", "more_fact", "show_lang",
];
pub const CALLBACK_PREFIX_COUNT : u8 = 6;
pub const CALLBACK_PREFIXES : [6]str = [
    "set_lang_", "sotd_next_", "sotd_prev_", "confirm_", "complete_", "reject_",
];
pub const EXACT_KEYS_ARE_TRIED_BEFORE_PREFIXES : bool = true;
pub const PREFIX_STRIP_KEEPS_THE_PAYLOAD_INTACT : bool = true;
pub const ROUTER_EXACT_KEY_COUNT_INCLUDING_PRE_ROUTER : u8 = 6;
pub const ROUTER_PREFIX_COUNT_INCLUDING_PRE_ROUTER : u8 = 7;

; Whether a press is ever measured against the cap at all. A press that matched stage 1 or 2 has
; already returned, so the answer is no, and every statement this file makes about the cap is a
; statement about the presses that get that far.
pub fn callback_reaches_the_length_check(matched_a_pre_router_branch: bool) bool {
    return !matched_a_pre_router_branch;
}

pub const ACTION_UNKNOWN : u8 = 0;
pub const ACTION_JOKE : u8 = 1;
pub const ACTION_FACT : u8 = 2;
pub const ACTION_SHOW_LANGUAGE_PICKER : u8 = 3;
pub const ACTION_SET_LANGUAGE : u8 = 4;
pub const ACTION_STRAIN_OF_DAY_PAGE : u8 = 5;
pub const ACTION_CONFIRM_ORDER : u8 = 6;
pub const ACTION_COMPLETE_ORDER : u8 = 7;
pub const ACTION_REJECT_ORDER : u8 = 8;
pub const CALLBACK_ACTION_COUNT : u8 = 9;

; src/bot/callbacks.rs:67-72, one requires_admin over the three order actions, enforced once at
; :227 rather than copy-pasted into three arms. The three actions advance an order; what they
; advance it TO is order-status's vocabulary and appears nowhere in this file.
pub const ORDER_ADVANCING_ACTION_COUNT : u8 = 3;
pub const CALLBACK_OWNS_NO_STATUS_VOCABULARY : bool = true;

pub fn callback_action_requires_admin(action: u8) bool {
    return action == ACTION_CONFIRM_ORDER or action == ACTION_COMPLETE_ORDER or action == ACTION_REJECT_ORDER;
}

pub fn admin_callback_action_count() u8 {
    var found : u8 = 0;
    var a : u8 = 0;
    while (a < CALLBACK_ACTION_COUNT) {
        if (callback_action_requires_admin(a)) {
            found += @as(u8, 1);
        }
        a += @as(u8, 1);
    }
    return found;
}

pub fn callback_data_is_within_cap(data_len: u16) bool {
    return data_len <= CALLBACK_DATA_MAX_LEN;
}

; Unknown is answered and never acted on (src/bot/callbacks.rs:57-58 declares it in those words,
; :109 produces it). The over-long case is answered and dropped at :173-180. Both refusals are
; silent to the customer by design, and both are refusals.
; SCOPE, stated because the invariants below would otherwise overclaim: this function models
; stages 3 to 5. A press that matched stage 1 or 2 never reaches it, which is exactly what
; callback_reaches_the_length_check is for.
pub fn callback_may_run(action: u8, within_cap: bool, id_is_configured_admin: bool) bool {
    if (!within_cap) {
        return false;
    }
    if (action == ACTION_UNKNOWN) {
        return false;
    }
    if (callback_action_requires_admin(action)) {
        return id_is_configured_admin;
    }
    return true;
}

; --- The escape rule -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; src/util.rs:2-6. Three characters, three replacements, and the order is the whole correctness
; argument: the ampersand must go first, because replacing the angle brackets first would leave
; their replacements' own ampersands to be replaced in turn and a less-than would ship doubly
; encoded. The double quote is NOT escaped, and that is right for this parse mode rather than an
; omission -- the bot never interpolates into an attribute.
pub const ESCAPED_CHAR_COUNT : u8 = 3;
pub const ESCAPED_CHARS : [3]str = ["&", "<", ">"];
pub const ESCAPE_REPLACEMENTS : [3]str = ["&amp;", "&lt;", "&gt;"];
pub const ESCAPE_AMPERSAND_INDEX : u8 = 0;
pub const ESCAPE_LT_INDEX : u8 = 1;
pub const ESCAPE_GT_INDEX : u8 = 2;

pub fn char_needs_escaping(c: str) bool {
    var i : u8 = 0;
    while (i < ESCAPED_CHARS.len) {
        if (ESCAPED_CHARS[i] == c) {
            return true;
        }
        i += @as(u8, 1);
    }
    return false;
}

pub fn escape_order_is_safe(ampersand_index: u8, lt_index: u8, gt_index: u8) bool {
    return ampersand_index < lt_index and ampersand_index < gt_index;
}

; WHAT HAPPENS IF A CALLER FORGETS. Nothing helps them. The helper is applied by hand at each
; interpolation and no wrapper applies it for anyone: across the three bot modules there are 23
; sends that declare HTML parse mode (src/bot/commands.rs 18, src/bot/callbacks.rs 3,
; src/bot/handlers.rs 2) and only 8 escape call sites guarding them. Repository-wide the figures
; are 40 and 40, but they are 40 in different files and pair up nowhere. No test asserts that an
; interpolated value was escaped before a send -- a grep of tests/ for html_escape returns 0. The
; failure is total rather than cosmetic: Telegram rejects the whole message, so the customer gets
; no text at all rather than text with stray markup.
; WHERE THE FAILURE GOES, corrected under review. A first draft of this file said the rejection
; is written at debug level because it is not one of the five strings src/bot/mod.rs:245-249
; treats as expected. That filter is tg_fire_and_forget's, and it is reached by very few of these
; sends: tg_fire_and_forget has exactly THREE call sites in the repository (src/bot/callbacks.rs
; :266, :303, :334) and only TWO of the 23 HTML sends sit behind one (:268 behind :266, :305
; behind :303). One more (:423) is discarded with .ok() and is not logged at all. The remaining
; twenty use .await? and hand the error out of the handler, where no error_handler of ours catches
; it -- the only one this repository installs (src/main.rs:704) is the update LISTENER's. So the
; honest statement is narrower and worse: for twenty-one of the twenty-three sends this file does
; not know where the rejection surfaces, and the five-string filter is not the answer.
pub const BOT_HTML_SEND_SITES : u8 = 23;
pub const BOT_ESCAPE_CALL_SITES : u8 = 8;
; Repository-wide, and the derivation matters because a reader re-running the grep gets 46, not
; 40: grep -rn "html_escape(" src/ returns 46 occurrences, six of which are in src/util.rs -- one
; is the definition at :2 and five are its own unit tests at :24, :31, :37, :44, :49. Forty are
; call sites elsewhere. They pair up with the 40 ParseMode::Html sends nowhere: the sends sit in
; ten files and the escapes in eleven, and the counts coincide by accident.
pub const REPO_ESCAPE_CALL_SITES : u8 = 40;
pub const REPO_ESCAPE_GREP_HITS_INCLUDING_THE_HELPER_AND_ITS_TESTS : u8 = 46;
pub const REPO_HTML_SEND_SITES : u8 = 40;
pub const FIRE_AND_FORGET_CALL_SITES : u8 = 3;
pub const BOT_HTML_SENDS_BEHIND_THE_EXPECTED_ERROR_FILTER : u8 = 2;
pub const A_TEST_ENFORCES_ESCAPING_BEFORE_SEND : bool = false;
pub const A_WRAPPER_ESCAPES_AUTOMATICALLY : bool = false;
pub const FORGOTTEN_ESCAPE_LOSES_THE_WHOLE_MESSAGE : bool = true;
pub const EXPECTED_SEND_ERROR_COUNT : u8 = 5;
pub const A_PARSE_FAILURE_IS_AN_EXPECTED_SEND_ERROR : bool = false;

pub fn message_renders_as_written(parse_mode_is_html: bool, text_is_escaped: bool) bool {
    if (!parse_mode_is_html) {
        return true;
    }
    return text_is_escaped;
}

pub fn unguarded_html_send_count(html_sends: u8, escape_sites: u8) u8 {
    if (escape_sites >= html_sends) {
        return 0;
    }
    return html_sends - escape_sites;
}

pub fn send_error_reaches_the_expected_error_filter(is_behind_fire_and_forget: bool) bool {
    return is_behind_fire_and_forget;
}

; CO-DECLARED, not duplicated. An earlier draft said this out loud "because a grep for
; html_escape over specs/ now returns two files"; that claim is FALSE as of 2026-09-21 and is
; corrected rather than quietly dropped. The re-run returns ONE file -- this one, and only in
; the three prose lines above that discuss the helper by name. The co-declarer spells the helper
; nowhere: it calls the thing an escaping pass and names no function. So the co-declaration is
; real but it is NOT greppable by the helper's name, which is precisely why the edge has to be
; carried by declaration names below instead of by a shared literal.
; Both citations into that file were RE-READ under review and both had drifted
; while it was being written; they had drifted AGAIN by 2026-09-21, which is why neither is a
; line number any more. The edge is declared by that file's REUSES_BOT_SURFACE_ID, its refusal
; to restate this rule is pinned by its ESCAPE_RULE_IS_RESTATED_HERE, and the six sites where an
; AI answer is escaped are carried by its OUTPUT_IS_ESCAPED_AT_EVERY_SITE. Five of those six sit inside the
; three bot modules counted above (src/bot/commands.rs:361 and :387, src/bot/callbacks.rs:262 and
; :300, src/bot/handlers.rs:79) and one is at src/promo.rs:609 outside them, which is why its six
; and this file's eight do not contradict each other. That file owns the claim that ONE caller
; escapes at every one of its sites; this file owns what the helper escapes, in what order, and
; what a caller who forgets gets. Neither restates the other: a grep of ai_assist.t27 for the
; escaped character set, the replacement strings and the word ampersand returns 0, RE-MEASURED
; 2026-09-21 and still 0.
